    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "yeah i checked the grafana dashboards during the last few failures and the metrics look pretty clean. no massive cpu spikes or memory leaks that would explain a sudden crash. it honestly feels like something is just... interfering with it, but we can't see it in the logs. is there any way to check for transient network glitches or maybe some weird background processes that aren't showing up in the main metrics?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "If the primary metrics look clean, you might be dealing with something subtle, like a transient network packet drop or a very brief, unlogged process interference. You should start monitoring network latency and packet loss *specifically* between the Jenkins agent and any external dependencies your unit tests rely on (databases, APIs, etc.). Use tools like `ping` or `mtr` running continuously during the test stage to catch those micro-glitches. Also, consider running the tests with a higher verbosity flag, if available, to see if the test framework itself logs any low-level connection warnings that might be getting swallowed.",
      "source": "target",
